or  $\frac{(x+y)(a-x-y) + xy}{xy(a-x-y)} = \frac{1}{a}$ ;

or  $a^2(x+y) - a(x+y)^2 + axy = xy(a-x-y)$ ;

or  $0 = (a-y)x^2 - (a-y)^2x - ay(a-y)$ ;

or  $0 = (a-y)[x^2 - (a-y)x - ay]$ ;

or  $y = a; x = \frac{(a-y) \pm \sqrt{(a-y)^2+4ay}}{2} = \frac{(a-y) \pm \sqrt{(a+y)^2}}{2} = \frac{(a-y) \pm (a+y)}{2}  \Rightarrow x = a, -y$ .

By deduction,  $x = -y \Rightarrow z = a$  for all  $y \neq 0$ .  Hence, at least one of  $x,y,z$  equals  $a$  as shown above.

