Inscribe a regular tetrahedron \(ABCD\) in the sphere and let \(O\) be its center. Let \(\Omega_A\) be the sphere with center \(A\) passing through \(B, C, D\); similarly define \(\Omega_B, \Omega_C\). These spheres intersect \(S\) through the blue circles in the figure. Their smaller arcs (electric blue) form a spheric triangle \(\Delta_0\) (with its interior) no two points of which lie on a distance greater than \(1\). Note that any subset of \(S\) not contained in a copy of \(\Delta_0\) contains two points apart more than \(1\).

Consider the green circles with  center \(O\) passing through \(B\) and \(C\), \(C\) and \(A\), \(A\) and \(B\). Their smaller arcs (light green) form a spheric triangle \(\Delta_1\). Note that due to symmetry \(S\) can be covered by exactly four copies of \(\Delta_1\). Also note that \(\Delta_0 \subsetneq \Delta_1\), so \([\Delta_0] < [\Delta_1]=[S]/4\).

Now suppose that \(S\) is partitioned into the sets \(S_0, S_1, S_2, S_3\) and contrary to the problem statement no one of them contains two points at distance more than \(1\). But then each \(S_i\) can be covered by a copy of \(\Delta_0\), whence \([S] \le 4[\Delta_0] < 4[\Delta_1]=S\). This contradiction finishes the proof.
