Supposing that  $P(n)=a_kx^k+a_{k-1}x^{k-1}+\dots+a_1x+a_0,$  we have
\begin{align*}P(7)=P(5+2) &= \sum_{i=0}^ka_i\left(5+2\right)^i=\sum_{i=0}^ka_i\left(\sum_{j=0}^i5^j\cdot2^{i-j}\cdot\binom{i}{j}\right).\end{align*}
We see that every term in the inner summation with  $j>0$  is divisible by  $5,$  so taking mod  $5,$  those all disappear:  $$ P(7)\equiv\sum_{i=0}^ka_i\cdot2^i=P(2)\equiv0\pmod{5}. $$  Likewise, every term in the inner summation with  $j<i$  is divisible by  $2,$  so taking mod  $2$  causes those to disappear:  $$ P(7)\equiv\sum_{i=0}^ka_i\cdot5^i=P(5)\equiv0\pmod{2}. $$  Hence  $P(7)$  is divisible by  $2$  and  $5,$  and so it is divisible by  $10.$

