First, we’ll prove that  $AP=PD$ . Let  $D$  denote the point diametrically opposite to  $B$  on  $(ABC)$ , which has the coordinates  $D=(a^2 S_A:-S_{AC}:c^2 S_C)$ .
By  $M=AI_{b}\cap CD$  and computation,  $$ M=(a^2 b:-bS_C:cS_C) $$  Since  $P=AO\cap BM$ , we realize that  $$ P=(a^2 bc:b^2 S_B:c^2 S_C) $$  It is obvious that  $D=(0:b:c)$ . Denote  $k=a^2 bc+b^2 S_B+c^2 S_C$ , then by barycentric distance formula and  $S_B+S_C=a^2$ ,
\begin{align*}
-AP^2=\frac{a^2b^2c^2 S_{BC}}{k^2}-\frac{b^2c^2 S_C (b^2 S_B+c^2 S_C)}{k^2}-\frac{b^2c^2 S_B (b^2 S_B+c^2 S_C)}{k^2}
=\frac{a^2b^2c^2}{k^2} (S_{BC}-b^2S_B-c^2S_C)
\end{align*}
and
\begin{align*}
-PD^2=\frac{a^2bc}{k^2} \left[\underbrace{\left( bS_B-\frac{k}{b+c} \right)\left( cS_C-\frac{k}{b+c} \right)+b^2 c\left( cS_C-\frac{k}{b+c}\right)+bc^2\left( bS_B-\frac{k}{b+c} \right)}_{=\ell} \right]
\end{align*}
By computation,
\begin{align*}
\ell=bcS_{BC}+b^2c^2S_B+b^2c^2 S_C-\frac{k}{b+c} \left(bS_B+cS_C+b^2 c+bc^2-\frac{k}{b+c}\right)
=bc(S_{BC}-b^2S_B-c^2S_C)
\end{align*}
which proves that  $AP=PD$ .
